.TH ps "" "Command"
.SH NAME
ps \- print process status
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBps [\-adflmnrtwx] [\-c \fIsystem\fB] [\-k \fImem\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ps -- print process status
Usage:	ps [ -afglmnrtwx ] [ -c system ] [ -k mem ]
Options:
	-a	Print all terminals' processes
	-c	Next argument is the system (default: '/coherent')
	-f	Put '-' in null fields for placeholders
	-g	Give the group leader for this process
	-k	Next argument is the memory image (default: '/dev/mem')
	-l	Print long format
	-m	Print scheduling information
	-n	No header line
	-r	Give the real size of the the process
	-t	Print CPU times
	-w	Wide column format (132 columns instead of default 80)
	-x	Print processes with no controlling tty
.HE
.B ps
prints one line of status for each process, reading the running
kernel's process table out of
.I mem
by way of the namelist in
.IR system .
Normally
.B ps
prints only processes attached to the caller's own controlling
terminal; the fields always printed are
.B TTY
(controlling terminal, or
.B ??
if none) and
.BR PID .
.PP
.IP \fB\-a\fR
Print processes attached to any terminal, not only the caller's.
.IP "\fB\-c \fIsystem\fR"
Take the kernel namelist from
.I system
instead of the default,
.BR /coherent .
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Precede each line with the process's queue-entry address, in octal.
This option is implemented by this
.B ps
but is not listed in the quick-help text above; see NOTES.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Print a
.B \-
placeholder in fields that would otherwise be blank, so
field-oriented filters can split the output uniformly.
.IP "\fB\-k \fImem\fR"
Take the memory image from
.I mem
instead of the default,
.BR /dev/mem .
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Long format: also print
.BR PPID ,
.BR UID ,
process size in K,
the flag word, the run state, and, for a sleeping process, the event
it is waiting on.
.IP \fB\-m\fR
Also print the scheduling fields
.BR CVAL ", " SVAL ", " IVAL ", and " RVAL ,
the process's current CPU schedule value, swap schedule value,
importance value, and response value.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Suppress the header line.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Print the real size of the process, including its user and auxiliary
segments (normally excluded, since those are shared with other
processes of the same user).
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Also print the elapsed CPU time fields
.B UTIME
and
.BR STIME .
.IP \fB\-w\fR
Wide format: use 132 columns for the command line instead of 80.
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Also print processes with no controlling terminal.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 14n
\fB/coherent\fR	default namelist (\fB\-c\fR)
\fB/dev/mem\fR	default memory image (\fB\-k\fR)
\fB/dev/kmem\fR	kernel data memory
\fB/dev/swap\fR	swap device
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR kill ,
.B sync
.SH NOTES
This
.B ps
does not implement a
.B \-g
option, although the quick-help text above (taken from this system's
.B /etc/helpfile
entry) lists one; giving
.B \-g
is rejected as a bad option.
Conversely, this
.B ps
implements a
.B \-d
debug option that the quick-help text does not mention.
Both discrepancies are in the shipped
.I /etc/helpfile
text, not in this page.
.PP
A process can change its own command name and arguments, so the
command line
.B ps
prints for it may not reflect what it is actually running.
The process table changes while
.B ps
is running, so its output is only a snapshot.
